# Required
SERVER_IP=

# Optional (defaults shown)
SSH_USER=ubuntu
SSH_KEY=
REMOTE_DIR=/home/ubuntu
SERVICE_NAME=domain
BINARY_NAME=domain
GOARCH_TARGET=amd64

# Optional. Name of the production database to back up (via pg_dump over
# SSH) before every deploy. Dumps land in server/backups/ (gitignored).
DB_NAME=portfolio

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime config (cmd/main.go)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

# Optional. Port the HTTP server listens on. Defaults to 8080. Only needed
# to run a second instance on the same host (e.g. scripts/deploy-friend.sh).
PORT=8080

# Required. Fixed password gating the domain area — checked against the
# POST /api/auth/login request body.
DOMAIN_PASSWORD=

# Required. Signing secret for the JWTs issued on login. Generate one with,
# e.g., `openssl rand -base64 32`.
JWT_SECRET=

# Required. Postgres connection string. When running via
# `docker compose up` at the repo root, this matches the `postgres` service
# defined there. From inside the compose network (i.e. the `server`
# container) the host is "postgres"; connecting from your own machine
# (e.g. psql) instead, use "localhost:5433" — the compose file maps the
# container's 5432 to host 5433 to avoid clashing with any other local
# Postgres on 5432.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://portfolio:portfolio@localhost:5433/portfolio?sslmode=disable

# Optional. CORS origin allowed to call the API. Defaults to "*".
ALLOWED_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Spotify integration (internal/spotify)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The domain area has exactly one Spotify connection (no per-user auth to
# key multiple by — see DOMAIN_PASSWORD above), authorized once via the
# "Connect Spotify" widget and refreshed server-side after that.

# Required. From your app at developer.spotify.com/dashboard.
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Required. Must exactly match a Redirect URI registered on that Spotify
# app. Local dev example: http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/spotify/callback
SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URI=

# Required. Where the browser lands after the OAuth round-trip finishes,
# success or failure (?spotify=connected / ?spotify=error is appended) — a
# route already behind RequireAuth in domain-client, e.g.
# http://localhost:5173/settings
SPOTIFY_FRONTEND_REDIRECT_URL=

# Required. Base64-encoded 32-byte key used to encrypt the stored Spotify
# refresh token at rest (kept separate from JWT_SECRET, which only signs —
# it never protects data sitting in the database). Generate with:
# openssl rand -base64 32
SPOTIFY_TOKEN_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# CMS publishing (internal/cms)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The CMS lives in the domain area and edits the PUBLIC site's content
# (projects, experience, writings, summary) as a draft. "Publish" serializes
# the draft to content.json and ships it to the public site's S3 origin +
# invalidates CloudFront. With CMS_S3_BUCKET unset, the CMS still works but
# POST /api/cms/publish returns 409 "publishing is not configured" — fine
# for local dev.
#
# IAM needed: s3:PutObject on arn:aws:s3:::<bucket>/<prefix>/* and
# cloudfront:CreateInvalidation on the distribution. Prefer an EC2 instance
# role; otherwise set AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY here.

# S3 bucket that serves the public portfolio site (same one deploy.sh
# syncs to). Empty = publishing disabled.
CMS_S3_BUCKET=

# Key prefix inside that bucket — must match the CloudFront origin path and
# portfolio-client's S3_PREFIX. Default "sat0ru".
CMS_S3_PREFIX=sat0ru

# Basename of the live document. Default "content.json" (the public site
# fetches "/content.json").
CMS_CONTENT_KEY=content.json

# CloudFront distribution serving the public site. Empty = skip
# invalidation (the object still updates; edges just revalidate on their
# own cache TTL).
CMS_CLOUDFRONT_DISTRIBUTION_ID=

# Region of the bucket. Also used for the CloudFront client.
AWS_REGION=

# Only if not using an instance role:
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Document Storage (internal/document)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# The domain area's file manager. Documents are uploaded and downloaded by
# the browser directly against a blob store using short-lived pre-signed
# URLs — the bytes never pass through this server.
#
# With DOCUMENTS_S3_BUCKET set, that store is S3: create a PRIVATE bucket
# (Block Public Access on) and give it a CORS rule allowing PUT + GET from
# the domain-client origin, e.g.
#   [{"AllowedMethods":["PUT","GET"],"AllowedOrigins":["https://<domain-client-origin>"],
#     "AllowedHeaders":["*"],"ExposeHeaders":["ETag"]}]
# IAM needed: s3:PutObject, s3:GetObject, s3:DeleteObject, s3:ListBucket on
# arn:aws:s3:::<bucket>/<prefix>/* (uses AWS_REGION / the creds above).
DOCUMENTS_S3_BUCKET=

# Key prefix for document objects inside that bucket. Default "documents".
DOCUMENTS_S3_PREFIX=documents

# With DOCUMENTS_S3_BUCKET unset (local dev, no-AWS deploy), bytes are kept
# on local disk here and served back through this server's
# /api/documents/_blob/ endpoint (HMAC-signed, no bearer token needed).
DOCUMENTS_LOCAL_DIR=./.data/documents

# This server's externally reachable base URL. Only used by the local-disk
# store, to build the signed blob URLs the browser hits. Default
# http://localhost:8080.
PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080
